--------------------------------------
WHAT THIS BOARD KNOWS

Would you like to know more? You would.

THE LINK IS NOT ENCRYPTED
You are connected over telnet. Telnet has
no encryption. Everything you type crosses
the network as readable text: your
password, your messages, all of it.

Anyone on the path can read it. Whoever
runs the wifi you are on, your internet
provider, the sysop's provider, anyone
between. That is not a bug here. It is the
price of letting a 1982 computer call in,
because a C64 cannot do encryption.

YOUR PASSWORD ON THE BOARD
It is never stored as you typed it. It is
salted and hashed with SHA-256, a thousand
rounds, and only the hash is written down.
Nobody can read it back, including the
sysop. Backups redact it.

That protects the file. It does nothing
about the wire, where you typed it in the
clear on the way here.

WHAT THE SYSOP CAN SEE
Your handle, your address, when you called
and for how long, and the last command you
ran. Staff can watch a node live. Messages
you leave sit in a file on the board until
they are read. Assume the sysop can read
anything on their own machine, because
they can. It is their machine.

SO WHAT IS MY REAL RISK
Honestly? Small. This is a hobby board on
a five dollar chip. It is conversation and
nothing critical. There is no money here,
no card numbers, no keys to anything. If
somebody read every word of it they would
learn what you said in a chat room.

There is one real risk, and it is this:
a password you use somewhere else.

If you reuse one and it is read on the
way here, the person reading it now has a
password that works on your email. That is
how accounts get taken, and it has nothing
to do with this board being good or bad.

SO
Use a password you use nowhere else.
Make it up here, forget it afterwards. Say
nothing on the board you would mind being
read out. Then enjoy yourself, because
that is what it is for.

Sysops: do staff work from your own
network or a VPN, never across the
internet. See PUBLIC.md.
--------------------------------------
